The common cold is a contagious viral infection of the upper respiratory tract. Symptoms typically include sneezing, nasal congestion, a sore throat, and a mild cough. Most cases resolve without treatment within one to two weeks.

Transmission occurs through respiratory droplets or by contact with contaminated surfaces. Rest, hydration, and over-the-counter medications may help relieve symptoms, although no cure exists for the infection.

Complications are uncommon in healthy individuals but may occur in people with weakened immune systems or chronic respiratory conditions. Good hand hygiene and avoiding close contact with infected individuals remain the most effective methods of prevention.|Multicellular division is a rare disorder characterized by an abnormally accelerated rate of cellular regeneration. Minor injuries may heal rapidly as damaged tissue is replaced at an unusually high speed, often with little or no visible scarring.

The underlying mechanism is poorly understood and appears to involve uncontrolled stimulation of normal tissue repair pathways. Despite its regenerative effects, prolonged or excessive cellular activity may place significant strain on the body's metabolic resources.

Long-term prognosis remains uncertain. Continuous monitoring is recommended, as abnormal rates of cell division may increase the risk of developmental abnormalities or uncontrolled tissue growth.|Asthma is a chronic respiratory disorder characterized by inflammation and narrowing of the airways. During an episode, breathing becomes more difficult, often resulting in wheezing, chest tightness, persistent coughing, and shortness of breath. Physical exertion commonly worsens symptoms.

The condition may be triggered by allergens, respiratory infections, cold air, or strenuous exercise. Although symptoms can vary in severity, untreated asthma may significantly reduce an individual's tolerance for sustained physical activity.

There is no permanent cure for asthma. Long-term management typically involves avoiding known triggers and the use of prescribed medications to maintain normal airway function and reduce the frequency of attacks.|Cardiac arrest is a sudden loss of effective heart function resulting in the cessation of normal blood circulation. Without immediate medical intervention, vital organs are deprived of oxygen, leading to rapid loss of consciousness and severe physiological impairment.

Cardiac arrest may occur as a result of underlying heart disease, electrical abnormalities, traumatic injury, or other serious medical conditions. Early recognition and prompt emergency treatment are critical to improving the likelihood of recovery.

Immediate treatment typically involves cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and defibrillation when appropriate. Survival depends heavily on the speed and effectiveness of medical intervention.|Muscle mutation is a rare condition characterized by an abnormal increase in muscle growth and tissue regeneration. Individuals affected by the disorder may experience a temporary increase in strength and physical endurance, often without an immediate explanation.

The accelerated growth places considerable stress on the body's metabolic and structural systems. As the condition progresses, normal muscle tissue may become unstable, leading to progressive weakness, reduced mobility, and long-term deterioration of physical performance.

No established treatment is currently available. Medical observation is recommended, as the apparent benefits of the disorder may be followed by permanent impairment of normal muscular function.|Color vision deficiency is a disorder that reduces the ability to distinguish certain colors. The most common forms affect the perception of red and green, while less common forms impair blue and yellow discrimination. Complete absence of color vision is extremely rare.

Most cases are inherited and remain stable throughout life, although eye injuries or neurological disorders may produce similar symptoms. Diagnosis is performed using standardized color vision tests administered by an eye care professional.

There is no permanent cure for inherited color vision deficiency. Specialized tinted lenses may improve color contrast for some individuals, but they do not restore normal color perception.
